\[ R_g^2 = \frac{1}{N} \sum_i |r_i - r_{\text{cm}}|^2 \] <pre><code>/* LAB 06-02  bio_geometry/rmsd.c */
vec cm = {0, 0, 0};
for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) { cm = add(cm, a[i].pos); }
cm = scale(cm, 1.0 / (double)n);

double sum = 0.0;
for (size_t i = 0; i &lt; n; ++i) { sum += dist2(&amp;a[i], &amp;cm_atom); }
double rg = sqrt(sum / n);
</code></pre> <p>Two passes: one for the centroid, one for the sum. The centroid must be computed before the distances, which is why the formula is written as a squared distance to a centroid rather than expanded — the expansion \(\sum |r_i|^2 - N|r_{\text{cm}}|^2\) is algebraically equal and numerically worse for large coordinates, because the two terms are large and their difference is small.</p> <p><code>1UBQ</code>'s \(R_g\) is about 11.7 Å, which is a number worth memorising as a sanity check for a parser: if the computed value is 117 or 1.17, the units or the indexing is wrong.</p>

\[ \cos\theta = \frac{(r_1 - r_2) \cdot (r_3 - r_2)}{|r_1 - r_2|\,|r_3 - r_2|} \] <pre><code>double c = dot(u, v) / (norm(u) * norm(v));
if (c &gt; 1.0) { c = 1.0; } else if (c &lt; -1.0) { c = -1.0; }
double theta = acos(c);
</code></pre> <p>The clamp is not optional. Computed in floating point, the cosine of a nearly straight angle can come out as <code>1.0000000000000002</code>, and <code>acos</code> of that is NaN — not an error, a quiet NaN that propagates through every subsequent sum and turns the final answer into <code>nan</code>. The reference program asserts that every angle it prints is finite, which is the only reliable detector because a NaN does not compare equal to anything.</p> <p>The alternative that avoids the clamp entirely is <code>atan2(|u × v|, u · v)</code>, which is well-conditioned for all angles and needs no branch. Both are correct; the <code>acos</code> form is what most code uses, and the clamp is what most code forgets.</p>

Algorithm Average Worst Stable In place Selection sort O(n²) O(n²) no yes Insertion sort O(n²) O(n²) yes yes Quicksort O(n log n) O(n²) no yes (with a stack) <p>The table is not the interesting part; the measurements are. Selection sort performs about <code>n²/2</code> comparisons regardless of the input, so a sorted array and a reversed array take the same time. Insertion sort performs <code>n²/4</code> comparisons on average and <code>n</code> on already-sorted input, which is why it is the right choice for small or nearly sorted arrays and why quicksort implementations switch to it below a threshold.</p> <pre><code>/* LAB 14-01  sorting/01_sort_bench.c */
#include "craft_bench.h"
#include "craft_assert.h"

static void selection_sort(int *a, int n) {
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; n - 1; ++i) {
        int min = i;
        for (int j = i + 1; j &lt; n; ++j) {
            if (a[j] &lt; a[min]) { min = j; }
        }
        if (min != i) { int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[min]; a[min] = t; }
    }
}
</code></pre> <p>Two details in that body matter beyond the algorithm. The swap is outside the inner loop, so the inner loop is a pure comparison scan; the original code in the archive swapped inside, which turned each comparison into a conditional store and made the already-slow algorithm slower. And the <code>min != i</code> check avoids a self-swap, which is harmless for <code>int</code> and not harmless for a large struct.</p>

Operation What it invalidates <code>push_back</code> that reallocates every iterator, pointer and reference into the vector <code>insert</code> / <code>erase</code> in the middle every iterator at or after the position <code>reserve</code> every iterator, pointer and reference <code>resize</code> smaller iterators past the new end <pre><code>for (auto it = v.begin(); it != v.end(); ++it) {
    if (*it == 3) { v.erase(it); }        // UB: it is invalid after erase
}

v.erase(std::remove(v.begin(), v.end(), 3), v.end());   // the correct idiom
</code></pre> <p>The erase-remove idiom is covered again in the algorithms chapter, because it is a library idiom rather than a container feature. The reason it works: <code>remove</code> moves the kept elements to the front and returns the new logical end, and <code>erase</code> then destroys the tail in one call.</p> <p>The classic defect — <code>v.push_back(x)</code> inside a range-for over <code>v</code> — is undefined behaviour that often appears to work, because the reallocation moved the block but the stale iterator still points at freed memory that has not yet been reused. AddressSanitizer turns it into a heap-use-after-free, which is why the labs run under it.</p>

Error codes Exceptions Cost when no error a branch zero on the hot path Cost when an error a branch at every call site unwinding, which is slow Ignorable by accident yes no Works across C ABI yes no Works in a destructor yes dangerous <p>The last two rows are the reason both exist. The C-facing boundary — a function exported to a C library, a callback registered with a C API — must not throw across it, because the C caller has no unwinder. The pattern is a <code>noexcept</code> wrapper that translates an exception into an error code:</p> <pre><code>extern "C" int c_run(const char *path) noexcept {
    try { run(path); return 0; }
    catch (const std::exception &amp;) { return -1; }
}
</code></pre>

Operator Usually Why <code>+=</code>, <code>-=</code>, <code>[]</code>, <code>()</code> member it modifies or accesses the left operand <code>+</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>*</code>, <code>/</code> non-member, often <code>friend</code> it returns a new object and treats both sides symmetrically <code>==</code>, <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&lt;=&gt;</code> non-member symmetry, and implicit conversion on both sides <code>&lt;&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;&gt;</code> for streams non-member the left operand is a stream, not your type <p>The rule behind the table is that a member function's left operand must already be of the class type. <code>a + 2</code> can be a member (<code>a.operator+(2)</code>), but <code>2 + a</code> cannot, unless a non-member overload exists. For symmetric arithmetic, the non-member form is what makes both orders work.</p> <pre><code>// LAB 06-01  cpp_operators/01_fraction_ops.cpp
class Fraction {
public:
    Fraction(long num, long den);
    Fraction operator+(const Fraction &amp;) const;      // member: left operand is *this
    Fraction &amp;operator+=(const Fraction &amp;);          // member: modifies *this
    friend Fraction operator*(Fraction, const Fraction &amp;);  // symmetric
    ...
private:
    long num_, den_;
    void normalise();
};
</code></pre>

Function Signature Generated when default ctor <code>T()</code> no other ctor declared copy ctor <code>T(const T &amp;)</code> no move declared copy assign <code>T &amp;operator=(const T &amp;)</code> no move declared move ctor <code>T(T &amp;&amp;) noexcept</code> no copy, move or dtor declared move assign <code>T &amp;operator=(T &amp;&amp;) noexcept</code> same destructor <code>~T()</code> always <p>Declaring any one of the copy or move operations suppresses the others; declaring a destructor suppresses the moves. The rule of five: if you need one, you probably need all five, or you should need none because your members are already RAII types.</p> <pre><code>struct Rule {              // the recommended default: none of the five
    std::vector&lt;int&gt; v;    // copyable, movable, destructible, all correct
};
</code></pre>
